latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify all black regions in the input grid:\n   a. Implement a depth-first search (DFS) or breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to find connected black cells.\n   b. Store each black region as a list of coordinates.\n\n2. For each black region, extract and analyze the pattern:\n   a. Determine the bounding box of the region.\n   b. Create a new grid representing just this region.\n   c. Count the number of non-black cells.\n   d. Identify the unique colors used (excluding black).\n   e. Calculate the maximum distance of any colored cell from the region's edges.\n\n3. Calculate a complexity score for each pattern:\n   a. Multiply the number of non-black cells, the number of unique colors, and the maximum distance.\n   b. This score balances the amount of information, color variety, and pattern spread.\n\n4. Select the most complex pattern:\n   a. Compare the complexity scores of all extracted patterns.\n   b. Choose the pattern with the highest score.\n\n5. Determine the optimal output grid size:\n   a. Start with the larger of the pattern's dimensions.\n   b. Ensure it's at least twice the maximum distance plus 3 (for centering).\n   c. Make sure it's at least 5x5 (minimum size requirement).\n   d. If the size is even, add 1 to make it odd for perfect centering.\n\n6. Center the chosen pattern in the new grid:\n   a. Create a new grid of the determined size, filled with black (0).\n   b. Calculate the padding needed to center the pattern.\n   c. Copy the pattern into the center of the new grid.\n\n7. Optimize the final grid size:\n   a. Trim any unnecessary black border, ensuring at least one cell of black border remains.\n   b. If trimming results in even dimensions, expand to make them odd.\n   c. Ensure the final size is still at least 5x5.\n\n8. Return the optimized grid as the solution.\n\nThis plan addresses all the key aspects we identified in our reflection:\n- It finds all potential patterns within black regions.\n- It evaluates complexity based on multiple factors.\n- It ensures the most interesting pattern is chosen.\n- It centers the pattern and optimizes the grid size.\n- It maintains the required minimum size and odd dimensions.\n\nBy following this plan, we should be able to solve the challenge correctly for all the given test cases and potential edge cases."
